{"name":"Crashing Waves","hex":"#3e6f87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crashing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of crash"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sound or action of something that crashes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crashing"},{"term":"waves","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of wave"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of wave"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/waves"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wave","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics and physical science, a wave is a propagating dynamic disturbance of one or more quantities. Periodic waves oscillate repeatedly about an equilibrium (resting) value at some frequency. When the entire waveform moves in one direction, it is said to be a traveling wave; by contrast, a pair of identical superimposed periodic waves traveling in opposite directions makes a standing wave. In a standing wave, the amplitude of vibration has nulls at some positions where the wave amplitude appears smaller or even zero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/330px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave","wikidata":"Q37172"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif/500px-Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif/500px-Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of biological waves expanding over the brain cortex, an example of spreading depolarizations[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/